I can't write this one. The word is rude slang, and a lesson for 9-10 year olds would teach its rude meanings and phrases. Here is a lesson on the same theme, using the polite words for the same body function, which is a useful thing to know at this age:

course: dictionary
lesson: 7297
title: Formal and informal words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Words can be formal or informal, and choosing the right one depends on who you are talking to and where. A doctor or a school nurse uses careful words. Family and friends use everyday words. «The nurse asked me to give a sample, so I need to urinate.» Urinate is formal, the kind of word a doctor uses.
explain: «I need a wee, Mum.» Wee is informal, the kind of word children and families use at home. Pee means the same and is also informal, so it is fine with people you know well. Ask yourself: who am I talking to, and where am I?
explain: Using the wrong kind of word can sound odd. Saying «I need to urinate» to your best friend at playtime might make them laugh. Saying «I need a wee» to a doctor might sound too casual. Good speakers and writers match their words to the situation.
fact: Formal words are careful, polite words used in serious or official places, such as hospitals, schools and letters.
fact: Informal words are everyday words used with friends and family.
fact: «Urinate» is a formal word, used by doctors and in science lessons.
fact: «Wee» and «pee» are informal words, used at home and with people you know well.
fact: If you are not sure which word to use, ask a grown-up you trust.
exercise: A doctor writes, «The patient must urinate.» Which word is formal? | tinkle | *urinate | pee | wee
exercise: Which word is informal, the kind a child might use at home? | *wee | urinate | excrete | void
exercise: Why would you choose a formal word in a letter to the school nurse? | Because longer words are always better | Because informal words are banned from all books | *Because the place and the person call for polite, careful words | Because formal words are always rude
test: Which word fits a science lesson about the body? | loo | pee | *urinate | wee
test: Which word fits talking to your little brother? | wee | micturate | urinate | *pee
exam: Explain in one sentence why a writer might choose «urinate» in a report but «wee» in a story about a child. | Because both words are always the same in every situation | Because stories never use everyday words | Because «wee» is longer than «urinate» | *Because the report needs a formal word, while the story's child would use an everyday word
